Protecting the Digital Doors and Windows: 
They constantly monitor the organization's internal network, looking for anything suspicious. If a threat tries to sneak in, they're usually the first to respond, like a security guard noticing an intruder. They also proactively test the "locks" and "windows" of the system by participating in activities like penetration testing or ethical hacking. This is like a security guard trying to break into their own building (with permission, of course!) to find weak spots before a real burglar does. They then suggest ways to make those weak spots stronger.

Preventing Trouble Before It Starts:
Security analysts don't just react to threats; they work to prevent them. They team up with IT professionals to install special software that helps identify risks and vulnerabilities. Think of this as installing advanced alarm systems and surveillance cameras to deter potential threats. They might also work with development teams to ensure that new software and hardware are built with security in mind from the very beginning, like an architect designing a building with strong security features already integrated.

Regular Security Check-ups: 
Just like you might get a regular check-up at the doctor, organizations need regular security check-ups. Security analysts conduct these "audits" by reviewing security records and activities. This helps them make sure that sensitive information, like passwords, isn't easily accessible to everyone, ensuring that only authorized people have access to important data.
